    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Algerian',
            subtitle: 'The Double-Deck Grand Patience',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Algerian (or Algerian Patience) is a grand, two-deck solitaire game noted for its complex layout and unique 'dual-direction' foundations. Unlike most solitaires that build in one direction, Algerian requires players to manage four foundations ascending from Ace and four descending from King simultaneously. With a wrapping tableau and a multi-stage redeal mechanic, it is a game of deep tactical planning and endurance.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "First documented by the author 'Tarbart' in the 1905 publication 'Games of Patience'. Its name suggests an exotic origin, though it is more likely an English invention from the Victorian/Edwardian era fascination with North African themes.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'One player only. Algerian is a solitaire (patience) game played by a single player.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two standard 52-card decks shuffled together (104 cards).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard tabletop or digital interface.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '1. **Foundations**: 8 slots (initially empty or 4 Aces/4 Kings placed if drawn). 2. **Tableau**: 8 depots with 1 card each. 3. **Reserve**: 6 piles with 4 cards each. 4. **Stock**: Remainder of the 104 cards.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Carthage: Similar to Algerian but with slightly different stock-dealing rules and no wrapping on the tableau.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
